prompt:
A photorealistic photograph of a street crossing in a residential district of Berlin on a bright, slightly overcast late morning, in a tall vertical picture, caught as it happens. Taken with a 35 mm lens from a first-floor window on the near side of the street, looking across and down onto the street, everything in sharp focus. Natural colours, soft daylight.

The first reference picture shows the young woman in three-quarter view, the second from the front: her face, her dark blonde hair tied back in a ponytail, her build and her height stay exactly as in them. Use them for the person only; her clothes here are a light-blue denim jacket over a white T-shirt and dark jeans.

Every surface in the picture is plain: the shop windows show only their goods, the awnings are plain cloth, the vehicles have plain bodywork and their number plates are turned away, the displays on the bus and the tram are dark, and every sign is a plain shape or a plain arrow. Each object stands clearly on its own, with bare pavement, road or wall around it, so that the eye can tell at once where everything is.

The picture is built in four bands, from the bottom edge to the top edge.

THE NEAR PAVEMENT, along the bottom edge of the picture, from left to right:
- a round kiosk with a dark-green roof and an open hatch, with shelves of drink bottles with plain coloured caps;
- a bright-yellow mailbox on a short post, plain on all sides;
- a street lamp, a tall dark-green pole with a lantern at the top;
- a traffic-light pole at the kerb, with its pedestrian light showing a lit red figure;
- a metal bicycle stand with two parked bicycles.

THE STREET runs across the whole picture from the left edge to the right edge, just above the near pavement:
- a red-painted cycle lane along the near side, with a cyclist in a yellow rain jacket riding along it towards the right;
- a zebra crossing of broad white stripes across the street in the middle of the picture, with two pedestrians halfway across it, a man with a backpack and an older woman with a shopping bag;
- on the left, a cream-coloured taxi with a plain yellow light box on its roof, stopped just before the zebra crossing;
- tram rails in the middle of the road, and a yellow tram on them on the right side of the picture, its front turned towards the left;
- a motorbike parked at the far kerb on the left.

THE FAR PAVEMENT, from left to right:
- a bus stop with a round plain yellow sign with a green rim on a pole, and a woman in a red coat waiting beside it; a white city bus has pulled in at the stop behind her;
- two parked cars in marked parking spaces, with a grey parking meter on the pavement beside them;
- a round blue traffic sign with a plain white arrow pointing right, on a pole at the corner;
- the crossing itself: on the right of the picture, a side street goes off straight away from us between the buildings, with a traffic light at its corner and a white delivery lorry driving into it.

THE BUILDINGS along the far side fill the upper part of the picture, plain cream and pale-ochre old Berlin facades with tall windows, four storeys high, under a strip of pale sky. On the ground floor, from left to right:
- a bakery: its wide window full of loaves of bread, rolls and brown pretzels on wooden shelves, and its glass door standing open;
- a pharmacy: a window with neat white boxes on glass shelves, and a plain green cross glowing above its door;
- a post office: a bright-yellow shopfront with a wide counter window, plain yellow above it;
- a small café on the corner of the side street, with three round tables and chairs on the pavement under a plain dark-red awning.

ANNA, the young woman of the reference pictures, stands on the far pavement right in front of the bakery, on its right-hand side, close to the open bakery door. She is seen in three-quarter view, holding her phone in front of her chest with both hands and looking down at its screen, her eyebrows drawn together: she is looking for her way. She has the ordinary height of an adult beside the bakery door, which rises well above her head, and she covers only the right-hand edge of the bakery window; the whole bakery door and the rest of the window stay visible.
